Use of TFEM
===========

I have developed TFEM for academic research (and also advanced courses) on
numerical methods in complex flows. I expect users to share code and/or
examples with other users by submitting these to me for possible inclusion
in TFEM. In this way we can all benefit from each others' work.

The use of TFEM is free of charge for academic researchers and students.
However, it is not open source software. It is not allowed to give TFEM to
others without my permission.

In order to be able to have all the decision rights, I will not allow multiple
copyright owners. Source code that goes in must have a single copyright
owner (me) or no copyright at all. Examples usually have no copyright.
Not all code that is developed together with TFEM needs to go in however,
but then it will not be maintained by me.

The vast majority of code has been developed by me. I have invested a
substantial amount of time in TFEM. Therefore, giving the source to someone
is not just one-way traffic. I expect something in return, from which our group
benefits in some way. For example, by being a coauthor on papers from work
we do together that involves TFEM. If you have permission to use TFEM for your
own research, at least add at an acknowledgment to a paper similar to:
"The authors thank M.A. Hulsen at the Eindhoven University of Technology (TU/e)
for access to the TFEM software libraries."

As has been stated above, TFEM has been primarily developed for academic
research. However, I will not explicitly rule out the possibility of
commercial use. In any case, the use of TFEM beyond university education
and research is not allowed without my permission.

Users of TFEM must agree with the rules as given above.

Martien Hulsen
m.a.hulsen@tue.nl

